## The problem

A worship band’s charts live in PDFs, screenshots and shared Google Docs. Change the key on Sunday morning and someone is re-exporting a PDF in the car park. Then the church Wi-Fi drops halfway through the set.

## Who it’s for

A worship leader with a guitar and a tablet, leading a band of three to seven people. No playback engineer, no Ableton rig: just the people on stage, who need the right chart in the right key.

## Decisions

**Offline first, not offline capable.** The whole app and the whole song library live on the device. Sync is a bonus, never a dependency, so the chart works at zero signal.

**Songs are Markdown files.** Each chart is a readable `.md` file with chords written inline. If the app disappeared tomorrow, every song would still open in any text editor. Ownership became a feature instead of a line in the terms.

**Dynamic, not static.** Transpose, add a capo, switch to Nashville numbers, go from one column to two: each is a single tap, with no PDF to regenerate.

**Churches first, personal stays.** I walked ten flows end to end, from first launch to Sunday morning. That review settled the model: a church is one shared space with no sub-groups, and a setlist *is* the service, not an “event” with a setlist attached.

**Guests need no account.** A substitute musician opens a shared setlist from a link and plays.

## Where it is

Live at setlistsmd.insideahead.com and in active development. A private soft launch with five to ten worship teams is planned for December 2026, so January can go to fixing what they find before the public beta on 1 February 2027.
